testing yupiter
===============

The tests are built and run with:

    ./configure
    make check

"make check" builds the library and every test program first, then runs
test/run-tests.sh.  The default build type is RelWithDebInfo, so the
ordinary run exercises the code the library ships with.  Configure with
--build-type=debug for an unoptimised run; both are covered by the
architecture workflows.

The runner prints one PASS or FAIL line per check and a summary, and
exits non-zero when any check failed:

    ...
    PASS install_and_consume

    20 checks, 0 failed

A single program can be built and run by hand:

    make build/test/test_heap
    ./build/test/test_heap

and the runner itself can be pointed at another build directory after
that directory has been built by name:

    make BUILD_DIR=build-debug check
    sh test/run-tests.sh build-debug


What is registered today
------------------------

    index_output          golden output of index.c
    format_output         golden output of format.c
    differential          format.c's engine against a reference C library,
                          case by case, at test time
    error_handling        failure paths (self-checking)
    stream_output         staging, discard, stream order and a payload
                          larger than the staging buffer, merged golden
                          output of stream.c
    large_write_is_reported
                          one 200000 byte write, checked for status,
                          length and bytes on a file and on a pipe
    short_write_retry     a scripted short write is retried to success
    string_checks         internal length, copy and fill helpers
    heap_checks           allocator, resizing, safe variants, leak counts
    heap_abort_overflow   safe size overflow terminates with 134
    heap_abort_oom        safe out of memory terminates with 134
    heap_abort_double_free
                          safe double free terminates with 134
    heap_abort_invalid_pointer
                          safe bad pointer terminates with 134
    heap_abort_realloc_overflow
                          safe resize overflow terminates with 134
    heap_abort_realloc_oom
                          safe resize exhaustion terminates with 134
    heap_abort_realloc_double_free
                          safe resize of a free block terminates with 134
    heap_abort_realloc_invalid_pointer
                          safe resize of a bad pointer terminates with 134
    index_cxx_output      the same golden output, from C++
    archive_is_freestanding
                          the built archive refers to no C library and
                          no C++ runtime, exports no mangled name and
                          holds no section that runs code before the
                          program; the shared object exports only the C
                          interface and names no library it needs
    install_and_consume   the library installs, and a separate project
                          builds and runs against the installation

The last check stages an installation into build/install-test, checks
that every promised file is there, then builds a consumer with
pkg-config and, when CMake is installed, with find_package(yupiter).
It is the only check that writes outside the test working directory,
and it rebuilds nothing: it installs the library the current build has
already produced.


The kinds of test
-----------------

The runner in test/run-tests.sh has one function per kind; the list
below names the function where that helps.

Golden output
    The program prints, the runner runs it and requires two things: an
    exit status of 0 and standard output identical to the golden file in
    test/.  A test that fails without printing anything is still
    reported, because the status is checked as well.

    Used by index_output, format_output and index_cxx_output
    (check_output).

Merged golden output
    The same, except that both streams are merged into one file before
    they are compared, so the golden file records where a diagnostic
    written to stderr lands relative to output written to stdout.

    Used by stream_output (check_merged).

Reference comparison
    One source is compiled twice: once linked against yupiter and once
    against the host C library, with -DYUPITER_REFERENCE choosing the
    calls.  The runner runs both programs and requires them to print the
    same bytes.  Nothing is stored, so the reference is a live one and
    cannot drift into being a snapshot of yupiter.

    Used by differential (test/differential.c, check_two).  Its cases
    are built at run time, so every combination of flags, width and
    precision is covered - over two thousand cases today - without the
    file growing with them.

Self-checking
    The program performs its own checks, prints "FAIL: ..." for each one
    that does not hold, and returns the number of failures as its exit
    status.  Zero is a pass, so no golden file is needed.

    Used by error_handling (test/errors.c), heap_checks (test/heap.c),
    string_checks (test/string.c) and short_write_retry
    (test/shortwrite.c).

Fatal paths
    A scenario that the library is documented to terminate with: the
    program names the scenario on the command line, does something
    invalid, and must end inside yupiter with status 134.  The runner
    asserts exactly that status, so a scenario that exits cleanly, exits
    with a different status or crashes fails the test instead of passing
    unnoticed.

    Used by the eight heap_abort_* tests (test/heap_abort.c,
    check_fatal).  The scenario names are listed in test/run-tests.sh;
    a new scenario is added there as well as in test/heap_abort.c.

Write destinations
    The program performs one large write and the runner runs it twice,
    once with standard output on a regular file and once with standard
    output on a pipe.  Each run has to report success, and the bytes that
    arrive have to be exactly the bytes the program produced, compared
    with the pattern the program uses rather than with a golden file of
    two hundred thousand bytes.

    Used by large_write_is_reported (test/largewrite.c,
    check_largewrite).

Mocked system call
    A short write cannot be produced on demand by a blocking descriptor,
    so test/shortwrite.c defines the three-argument syscall trampoline
    itself for the duration of each scenario.  The mock records each
    request, answers with a scripted short count or error, and forwards
    every call it does not script - including the final exit_group - to
    test/rawsyscall_<processor>.S, a copy of the real trampoline.  The
    program then checks the retry cursor, the byte sequence and the
    return status, and carries its own verdict.

    Used by short_write_retry (test/shortwrite.c).

Installation
    test/install-test.sh stages "make install DESTDIR=..." into
    build/install-test/stage, checks that the headers, the libraries and
    the package metadata are where the install rules promised to put
    them, then builds test/consumer/main.c twice: once with pkg-config
    and once with find_package(yupiter) when CMake is available.  Both
    consumers run and must print "consumed yupiter".  A missing
    pkg-config or CMake is reported as not exercised, so the remaining
    checks still run; the GitHub Actions runners provide both.

    Used by install_and_consume.

Archive inspection
    test/check-freestanding.sh runs nm and readelf over the built
    archive and requires three properties of every member: every
    undefined symbol is "main", the linker-defined
    "_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_", or a "yupiter"-prefixed symbol of the
    library, no exported symbol is outside the C interface (only
    "yupiter_*" and "_start"), and no member holds an ".init_array",
    ".ctors", ".tdata" or similar section.  A static library is never
    linked on its own, so inspecting it is the only way to prove this for
    every member instead of only for the members a particular link
    happens to need.  When the shared object is built, the same script
    requires it to have no undefined dynamic symbols, to export only
    the documented public declarations - an internal "yupiter_*" helper
    is a failure there - to name no NEEDED library and to hold no
    initialiser section.  The check fails rather than skips when nm or
    readelf is missing, so the property is never assumed.

    Used by archive_is_freestanding.


Golden files
------------

test/expected-format.txt was produced from test/format.c by a reference
C library rather than by yupiter itself, so the comparison is a real
check and not a snapshot of whatever the implementation happens to do.
Regenerate it from the repository root with a hosted compiler:

    cc -std=c17 -DYUPITER_REFERENCE -w test/format.c -o /tmp/reference
    /tmp/reference > test/expected-format.txt

test/expected-index.txt matches what index.c prints.

test/expected-stream.txt is different in kind: it is the merged output
of test/stream.c run against yupiter, and it states a property that
only yupiter defines - that stdout is flushed when a newline is staged
while stderr is unbuffered - so a reference C library is the wrong thing
to generate it from (glibc buffers stdout to a file and would interleave
the two streams differently).  Regenerate it with the same redirection
the test uses, checking the merged result before committing it:

    ./build/test/test_stream > /tmp/merged 2>&1
    cat /tmp/merged
    cp /tmp/merged test/expected-stream.txt

Regenerating any golden file changes what the library is held to:
review the diff before committing it.


Rules a new test follows
------------------------

  * A freestanding test is a C17 program in test/ that links
    build/libyupiter.a, the startup object of the target and no C
    runtime.  Its rule lives in mk/tests.mk; the pattern rule covers a
    test that needs nothing but its own source.  A test that needs an
    extra assembly source, a different language or a hosted link gets an
    explicit rule next to the pattern rule, and its name is added to
    FREESTANDING_TESTS or HOSTED_TESTS in mk/variables.mk.
  * The C tests use the warning policy the library is built with for C,
    plus -fno-builtin and -fno-stack-protector; the C++ test uses the
    C++ set and is linked as a hosted program, because a C++ program
    needs the C++ runtime for its own start-up.  The format check stays
    enabled everywhere.  test/format.c, test/errors.c and
    test/differential.c call the few cases that pin down behaviour the
    standard leaves undefined or that "-Wformat" rejects - the '0' flag
    on a string, a precision on %p, a '+' on an unsigned conversion, an
    empty format string, a width literal too large for an int - through
    a function pointer, so the attribute on the declaration does not
    reject the test while every ordinary call is still checked.  The
    reason is stated again where those cases are, in the files
    themselves.
  * A test that needs internals includes the internal header of that
    layer (include/yupiter_io.hxx or include/yupiter_mem.hxx) by name;
    the include directory is already on the path of every test.
    errors.c does this on purpose: a stream bound to a bad descriptor
    cannot be obtained through the public interface, and its
    sticky-error behaviour is worth testing.  stream.c uses the same
    header for yupiter_stream_discard, and largewrite.c for
    yupiter_sys_write.
  * A test that checks a value carries its own verdict (exit status), so
    it needs no golden file.
  * A program whose output is compared with a golden file writes nothing
    else to standard output; a note for a reader goes to standard error,
    which the merge in check_merged places where it belongs and the
    golden files record.
  * A new check is added to test/run-tests.sh with one of the existing
    helpers, and it prints PASS or FAIL through pass() or fail().  A
    check that cannot run must fail with a diagnostic; skipping is not a
    pass.


The leak policy of the heap tests
---------------------------------

The allocator tests use small allocations only, so anything a bug loses
stays small, and the accounting in include/yupiter_mem.hxx turns any leak
into a failing check: after every scenario the live byte and block
counts must be back at zero.  The exhaustion scenario deliberately fills
the arena to prove that a full heap behaves - yupiter_malloc reports it,
printf falls back to its stack buffer, and releasing everything restores
the whole arena.  The realloc scenarios cover growth into the free tail,
shrink with a split, an obstructed block that has to move, the null and
zero-size cases, an invalid pointer and the safe variant; heap_abort.c
adds the fatal overflow, exhaustion, double-free and invalid-pointer
failures of yupiter_realloc_safe.

The tests do not depend on the arena size: they ask the allocator what
it has and shape the scenario accordingly, so the same sources run
against any arena the build accepts.  Configuring with --heap-size=64
and running the same tests is a supported and useful variation, and the
configure guard rejects anything below 64.


What is not tested
------------------

  * short_write_retry scripts the result of the three-argument syscall
    trampoline; it proves the retry logic, not that a particular
    terminal or descriptor returns a short count in practice.  The real
    system-call path is covered by large_write_is_reported, which writes
    far more than the stream buffer to a file and to a pipe and checks
    the status, the length and every byte.
  * The count-versus-error boundary of yupiter_kernel_result is covered
    only through yupiter_sys_write.  yupiter_sys_read has the same
    classification and no caller yet; when the input layer arrives, its
    own test has to cover a read of 4096 bytes or more.
  * Sanitizers and valgrind are not run: a "-nostdlib" freestanding
    program has no runtime for them.  Leak detection uses the library's
    own accounting instead.  The hosted C++ test could be run under a
    sanitizer by hand, but the build does not do it.
  * Only the architectures a workflow builds the project for (Linux on
    aarch64 and on x86_64) are exercised.  No other operating system or
    architecture is claimed to work without a toolchain and a successful
    build.
  * The comparison against a reference C library covers the conversions
    both implement.  Where they are documented to differ, the difference
    is pinned by a golden file instead; see docs/formatting.
